      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalists often rely on \"original public meaning,\" examining founding-era legal materials and treatises to determine what a reasonably informed citizen would have understood the right to bear arms to mean, often emphasizing collective or militia-based interpretation. Living constitutionalists employ frameworks suggesting that constitutional texts possess an inherent capacity for adaptive meaning, pointing to subsequent amendments and evolving social norms as evidence of an enduring principle rather than a static historical concept. They argue that a rigid originalist reading fails to protect modern rights against outdated societal contexts.",
